*Go on...

Sam: On a basic level, you have your typical employee exploitation—ECHO operates in 155 countries, and lots of grunt work gets shoved off to remote locations. I have a Zoom recording of an Indonesian factory lead admitting they don’t give bathroom breaks and several workers have quit over work-related injuries.There’s also an old lawsuit that’s resurfaced over shady financial dealings.

Sam: What I find even more interesting, to be perfectly frank, are allegations of mind control through the WAVE chips. I first joined ECHO with big dreams of connecting people. At the founding, Paul Aquino promised that our technology would link mankind, facilitating communication and information sharing across countries with a speed never before seen from other devices. But in my line of work as a quality engineer, I grew to question those altruistic mission statements. 

Sam: I’m convinced the WAVE implant is a trojan horse for all other types of modifications, ultimately allowing ECHO to plant ideas that they want the public to support. I know people compare modern marketing and social media to mind control all the time, but this is a whole new level.

**Wow, that's a lot...

Sam: So, here’s the catch. In my eyes, the mind control story is the only one really worth pursuing. Poor work conditions and shady financial practices, what global corporation does not partake in that? If we water down mind control with all this boring business jargon, I worry it’ll undercut the story’s impact. If you want access to my exclusive information about the mind chips, I ask that you turn a blind eye to the rest. On the other hand, if you don’t care about that story, I’ll take it to someone else and you can write about the FCC and workers’ rights. Up to you.

***That's highly unusual...

Sam: If you mention rumors or allegations, fine. If you find the information on your own, fine. I’m just not willing to connect you with my informants if our priorities aren’t aligned.

