==meredith==
You choose to spend your limited time grabbing a drink with Meredith. She’s a senior software engineer who worked on early versions of the ECHO chip and now monitors technical aspects of the program. 

Meredith orders a double Old Fitz on ice. Smooth. With her southern drawl, you’re surprised she ever traded hot Texas days for the brutal northern winters.

“You wouldn’t even believe the things these ECHO chips are doing,” she starts before you even ask your first question. “Scanning people’s each and every thought, analyzing their emotions and sending it all to Aquinos’ servers.”

*People know ECHO records neural activity. What makes this different from any other social media that logs user behavior?

“This isn’t just monitoring,” Meredith glances around. “It’s veering into full-on mind control.”

“The waves run both ways,” she continues. “After ECHO knows enough about you, it clumps personality types into clusters available to marketing agencies. Companies can pay to access “influential thinking” for a carefully-chosen group of user profiles—aka, subliminal messages in the form of brain activity encouraging purchases of a given product.”

**Why haven’t I heard about this?
“They don’t want you to. Companies disguise the payments as other forms of above-ground advertising. This whole thing is totally illegal, but with how aggressive advertisers are getting, their cover’s about to be blown.”

