==apathy==
Your article goes live Sunday morning and is front page news. You run the media circuit, giving interviews for cable news, podcasts and even The New York Times.

But the overwhelming response? ECHO users don't care.

Follow up surveys reveal that most Americans don't really care whether someone is tracking and controlling each and every thought. As long as they can text their friends without lifting a finger and superimpose TV shows over boring real life meetings, they're willing to deal with some drawbacks.

"I don't even have that much money, so companies won't work that hard to influence me, personally," one user posts online. "And my thoughts are so generic, I'd share them with anyone!"

Tough luck. You ditch investigative reporting altogether and pivot to sports coverage to try and write stories that actually resonate with today's audience.
-> end

==two_months_later==
- [Two months later]
You successfully pitch your story to {outlet}. During the research and fact-checking process, their reporters ask to confirm Sam's identity. The lead news editor wants to meet with him in person to corroborate the story.

Surely Sam will be thrilled that his scoop is getting national attention.

When you call him up, the coldness in his voice catches you off-guard.

"Chloe... remember when I said I specifically wanted this article to run in The Kalamazoo Citizen? That wasn't just a suggestion. In order for a *real* paper to publish the story, they wouldn't just investigate ECHO. They would investigate me."

"You think I'm not a real reporter?" You snap back. "I've done my due  diligence. So what else are you hiding?"

Sam ends the call abruptly. You call back again.

Before you can get a single question in, he blurts, "I made it up. There." 

What?

"The whole mind-control thing. Is it technically true? No. Is it something they would start? Sure. You were already clever enough to know I wanted revenge, you were just too noble to imagine how far I'd go."

So ends your affiliation with {outlet}. Oh well, after all this drama, a slow week in Kalamazoo doesn't look half bad.
-> end

==whistleblower==
Your article sparks major public controversy. The president pledges to ban WAVE by the end of the month unless they sell to another owner. This ban is extremely unpopular with ECHO users, many of whom still don't see a major issue with using the chips.

"You can't take away our brainway memes!" becomes a rallying cry.

You've made an impact, but you have to delete your Twitter and Instagram due to the hoards of critics calling you out. They're encouraged by Paul Aquino, who posts daily TikTok edits of an embarrassing Halloween costume you wore back in middle school. You take a mental health break from journalism and hire a PR firm to minimize your digital footprint.

It's a tough personal blow, but you already miss the daily grind. You're in talks with BBC to restart your reporting career at a highly respected outlet across the pond. Watch out world, Chloe McCracken won't be on the sidelines for long.
-> end

==disinformation==
You play it too safe by sharing your allegations and giving ECHO a week to respond.

Within three days, a NYT story appears addressing rumors of mind control within WAVE. Paul Aquino claims that a rouge engineer by the name of Sam Hereford started feeding commands into the algorithim. This was reportedly an unknown feature that ECHO is working dilligently to patch. 

"ECHO is committed to free and independent thought," Aquino tells the NYT. "Rest assured that this type of oversight will not happen again."

You still publish your article, but the news barely registers. ECHO beat you to the punch.
-> end

==signal_ending==
That night in your hotel, your phone buzzes with a Signal connection request. From Paul Aquino.

Has to be a bot. Someone trying to trip you up with fake news. You accept just to make sure.

Two days later—Tuesday—at 4:28 p.m., you receive a notice that you were included in a Signal chat group. It was called the “ECHO small group.”

A message to the group, from “Paul Aquino” reads: “Team – establishing a principles [sic] group for coordination on ECHO update, particularly for over the next 72 hours. I fully share everyone's concerns about civil rights groups discovering our CLASSIFIED mind control plans—they are PATHETIC.”

This is far too evil-villain-coded to be real. You turn your phone off and head to sleep.

But exactly 72 hours later, ECHO rolls out a new update. You re-open the group chat, which now features hundreds of messsages outlining exactly how ECHO 5.0 has finally consolidated political and corporate power.

Armed with screenshots, you break the news in Kalamazoo's Sunday edition.

At first, it looks like the FTC and FCC won't even acknowledge the scandal. A month later, however, ECHO is permanently shut down. 

You're a national hero.
-> end

==discovery==
"What's going on??" you beg Sam. "The people at Hardage Farm recognized me. They said I was part of the chip implant group. And the scary part is, I feel like I'm starting to remember living here too."

Sam breathes out a heavy sigh.

"So the memory feature is buggy after all."

"Hello?" you plead, ear pressed to the phone.

"I hate that you had to find out this way, but we had to know. Yes, you were part of the initial ECHO group. When you opted to leave the study, the researchers performed the memory wipe and released you back home. But we've had reports of other past participants having leaky memories of this place. We needed to bring you back for that reason, to test if the issue could occur in all subjects."

"So this was all a ploy? Wait... do I still have a chip in my head?"

"Don't worry." You can almost hear Sam's toothy grin over the airwaves. "We've improved the technology since then. Now, we know you need the factory reset."

His voice seamlessly switches sources, from your phone's speaker to inside your mind itself, intersecting with your inner dialouge. 

"You won't remember any of this at all."
*[...]
->start

==libel==
You're jazzed up about this story. Everyone needs to know what WAVE is up to with these ECHO chips. And Paul Aquino is clearly just a horrible person.

You skim Sam's documents and then write the entire article in a caffine-induced haze, leaving spaces to insert expert testimony and quotes from employees later on.

When you share your draft with Tim, he asks whether you can back up all of your findings. "Sure" you brush him off, confident in your work.

It's easy enough to find disgruntled employees willing to speculate about ECHO's mind control efforts, and you also interview several renowned AI researchers to weight in about the technology and moral implications.

It's easy enough to report when you already know the final story.

A week after the article goes live, however, you recieve a cease and desist from WAVE industries. They claim the documents the article cites as fabricated.

At first, you aren't concerned. But months in court prove that Sam, who won't respond to any subpoena, set you up. There's no proof to support the biggest claims in your article, even though the pieces about Aquino's bad personality and reckless behavior are likely true.

You lose the defamation trial and owe WAVE $5 million.
-> end

==buyout==
WAVE's communications head, Leslie Hayes, welcomes you to his plush office. 

"I see you've gotten the wrong idea about what we're up to here at ECHO," he starts.

"These aren't just ideas. I have interviews and documents to back up these leads," you answer.

"Sure, sure. But the thing is-we can provide compelling evidence against all of that. And anyway, people really enjoy our product! Trust me, this isn't the hill you want to die on,' Hayes replies. 

"You know what's much more interesting? Shein is sending a group of influencers to a Chinese factory for a tour that will prove there's no labor law violations there. What do you say we cover all your travel costs to go report on that instead?"

You've always wanted to go to China. Maybe the Shein tour is PR fluff, but there's bound to be a real story going on there.

You begrudgingly accept, trading in the seemingly endless ECHO reporting for a trip to the Great Wall.
-> end

== finish ==
If this were a fully realized game, this section would be completed. But alas, due to other finals and timing contraints, you'll have to pick another path for now. -> start

== end ==
The end.
